          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Gregor Mendel</strong><br>\nGregor Mendel was born on July 22, 1822, in Heinzendorf, Austria. He grew up in a peasant family and often assisted in the family garden. Mendel showed early interest in plant breeding and nature. This interest eventually led him to choose monastic life at the Augustinian monastery in Brno, where he had access to education and research.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Education and Monastic Life</strong><br>\nMendel entered the Augustinian monastery in Brno in 1843, allowing him to study at the University of Vienna. He received a solid education in physics and mathematics. This academic foundation was crucial for his later experiments and theories on inheritance. As a monk, Mendel took the name Gregor and dedicated himself to teaching and science.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Experiments with Pea Plants</strong><br>\nFrom 1856 to 1863, Mendel conducted experiments with pea plants. He chose peas because they are easy to cultivate and exhibit clear traits, such as seed shape and color. Mendel planted thousands of peas and carefully observed the expression of their traits over several generations. His methodology was remarkably precise and systematic.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "es"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
