          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Mendel's Laws of Inheritance</strong><br>\nMendel's research led to the formulation of the basic laws of inheritance: the Law of Uniformity, the Law of Segregation, and the Law of Independent Assortment. He realized that traits are inherited through \"factors,\" which we now know as genes. His discovery laid the foundation for genetics as a science.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Challenges and Recognition</strong><br>\nDespite the innovation of his work, Mendel faced challenges when trying to publish his results and gain recognition from the scientific community. His research was largely ignored during his lifetime, as his ideas were ahead of their time, and the significance of the results was not recognized.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Later Years and Administrative Duties</strong><br>\nAfter his experimental years, Mendel increasingly devoted himself to administrative tasks in the monastery. He was eventually elected as the abbot and spent much of his time leading the monastery. In this role, he had less time for his research, which some historians consider a loss for science.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "es"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
