          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Rediscovery of His Work</strong><br>\nOnly after Mendel's death were his works rediscovered by other scientists. In the early 20th century, researchers began to understand the laws of inheritance and recognized the importance of Mendel's discoveries. His work was reevaluated and finally received the recognition it deserved.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Mendel's Legacy and Genetics</strong><br>\nToday, Mendel is regarded as the father of modern genetics. His methods and results are taught in schools and universities worldwide. The principles he uncovered are fundamental to our understanding of inheritance and biological diversity, with far-reaching applications in medicine, agriculture, and biotechnology.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Assessment of Mendel's Significance</strong><br>\nThe story of Gregor Mendel illustrates how an exceptional scientist can be ahead of his time. Mendel's patience, attention to detail, and analytical mind allowed him to decipher some of the most fundamental principles of life. His life's work remains a central lesson in the history of science.</p>\n"
              },
